>h2 Hosted Bootstrap Use
>tree open Hosted Bootstrap Use
The first CupidBuild implementation stays within the represented hosted C
profile. Checked CupidC builds its Linux and Windows command, policy, and
transaction modules. CupidBuild now parses the exact seed schema, target,
provenance, artifact roles, and Linux build plan before it runs a tool. The
current Windows and Linux command suites accept the expanded source. This adds
no new HolyC-style syntax and does not change the in-OS language surface.
The same hosted source now enumerates the seed directory, rejects unlisted
executable-shaped peers, and checks every frozen image against the selected
ELF32 or PE32 execution profile. It repeats membership checks after both
attempted checked commands, including failure and timeout paths.
The hosted i386 string runtime now supplies standard binary memchr so the same
CupidBuild source compiles and links through CupidC. The runtime probe checks
matching, absent, embedded-zero, unsigned-byte, and empty-range searches.
The current checked Toolchain run rebuilt all 22 hosted artifacts and passed
the full contract surface. Its Linux candidate proof freezes 58 inputs and
matches 22 C
objects, one startup object, and all six tool images between stages three and
four. Both CupidBuild images also pass the guarded object command and its
missing-source rollback. The six-tool cohorts are now active, and their
promoted-seed self-consumption reproof passes with all six initial images equal
to stage two on both platforms. This changes hosted bootstrap evidence, not
CupidC language behavior. CupidBuild owns the ISR, context-switch, bootloader,
and SMP-trampoline recipes, while Python retains coordination and publication
elsewhere.
Source-head CupidBuild now adds a native checked CupidObj runner without adding
language syntax. Linux creates no .cupidbuild-run namespace. It freezes the
manifest and six tools in fully sealed anonymous memfds and pins the working
directory by descriptor. The child calls fchdir before remapping captured
streams. A tool descriptor in slot 0, 1, or 2 is moved above the standard
descriptors before fexecve or execveat. The dup2, pipe read and write, and wait
loops retry EINTR; dup2 also retries EBUSY. The stream memfds are sealed before
reading. A close-on-exec launch-status pipe preserves a genuine exit of 125.
The static i386 startup supplies cupid_linux_syscall5.

Windows pins and rechecks the working-directory identity. Its private root and
files are handle-pinned, and it retains the tool without write or delete
sharing through CreateProcessA. It forwards captured streams in binary mode.
This preserves their exact bytes. Cleanup deletes a file that changed in place
when its identity still matches, but preserves a replacement with a different
identity. Both paths withhold captured output if the live cohort changes. Both
checked seeds carry the command, and Make uses it for every direct CupidObj
recipe.

The checked runner now admits CupidLD as well as CupidObj and rejects every
other tool name. A direct and checked fixed-address CupidLD link must produce
the same ELF bytes. Both source-head fixed points run checked CupidObj through
their stage-three and stage-four CupidBuild images, compare the relocatable
result, and check the invalid-option diagnostic. Linux has 25 failure, six
help, and 32 success cases. Native Windows has 14 failure, six help, and 19
success cases. Make now runs 186 ordinary CupidObj recipes through that checked
command without changing their output formats. Both normal kernel links use
the same checked runner.

The same represented hosted C profile now builds a typed CupidBuild JPEG
transaction and its independent native validator. Both source-head fixed-point
matrices run the command across consecutive CupidBuild images, compare the
relocatable result, and prove rollback for a progressive JPEG. Their source
inventories are 26/6/33 on Linux and 15/6/20 on native Windows. This is source
capability only; the active seeds and normal JPEG rule remain unchanged.

The Windows CupidBuild CLI suite completed 66 tests in 65.934 seconds with
three expected skips. The host-runner Python module completed eight tests in
0.962 seconds with four POSIX skips, and its dedicated Make contract passed.
All six CupidASM source tests passed in 3.771 seconds. Strict Windows and
freestanding i386 adapter compilation passed, as did timeout-and-seed-drift
precedence. After WSL restarted, the complete 129-case bootstrap-seed module
ran. It passed 126 cases; three final assertions still held stale transition
expectations. The corrected focused cases now pass. A complete Linux
fixed-point rerun passed in 2,158.113 seconds, and an independent native
Windows fixed-point rerun passed in 1,847.207 seconds. All 129 cases then
passed in 3,569.451 seconds.
>endtree

>h2 Purpose
>tree open Purpose
This page documents the HolyC-inspired features implemented in CupidC
(kernel/lang/cupidc.cc and related files) and records their behavior. The
normal build compiles cupidc.cc with the checked CupidC seed.

Try a quick REPL expression:
[Evaluate 42](repl:U32 answer = 42; answer;)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 1: Sized Type Keywords
>tree open Feature 1: Sized Type Keywords
Supported keywords:
  U0, U8, U16, U32, U64, I8, I16, I32, I64, Bool

Compatibility:
- Legacy int/char/void code continues to work.
- Bool is accepted as a language type token.
- long/short/signed/unsigned spellings are accepted for C compatibility.
- Current codegen still targets CupidOS's 32-bit flat ABI.

Example:
  U8 small = 255;
  U16 medium = 1000;
  U32 large = 100000;
  large + medium + small;

Demo: bin/feature1_types.cc
[Run feature1_types](shell:feature1_types)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 2: Top-Level Execution
>tree open Feature 2: Top-Level Execution
main is optional.

Both forms work:
  U32 x = 7;
  Print("x=%u\n", x);

and:
  void main() { ... }

If both exist, top-level statements run and then main() is called.

Demo: bin/feature2_top_level.cc
[Run feature2_top_level](shell:feature2_top_level)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 3: class Syntax + Methods
>tree open Feature 3: class Syntax + Methods
class is accepted as a struct-style data type with methods.

Method call forms supported:
  obj.Method(...)
  ptr->Method(...)

Methods compile with implicit self parameter lowering.

Example:
  class Point {
    I32 x;
    I32 y;
    I32 Sum() { return x + y; }
  };

  Point p;
  p.x = 3;
  p.y = 4;
  p.Sum();

Demo: bin/feature3_class.cc
[Run feature3_class](shell:feature3_class)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 4: Forward Function Calls
>tree open Feature 4: Forward Function Calls
Two-pass symbol registration supports calling functions before their bodies
appear in the file.

Example style:
  U32 A() { return B(); }
  U32 B() { return 42; }

Demo: bin/feature4_forward_calls.cc
[Run feature4_forward_calls](shell:feature4_forward_calls)
>endtree

>h2 Doom Compatibility
>tree open Doom Compatibility
CupidC has an explicit --doom-compat profile for the
vendored Doom source. It represents a bare call made before any declaration
as an old-style external int() function. Calls parsed before a later prototype
keep the usual default argument promotions. The profile also marks the eleven
audited conversions between unqualified function pointers and unqualified
four-byte data or void pointers. The i386 emitter keeps those pointer bits
unchanged.

Strict C and plain GNU mode still reject the implicit conversions, and
explicit function/data casts remain outside Linear IR. One-active-member
union initialization and ordinary narrow bit-field promotion close the other
two audited source boundaries. The checked seed now emits all 80 Doom-tree
objects.

The production wrapper also emits the three compatibility roots. It preserves
the explicit static string cast in doom_libc_stubs.cc and emits the exact
dg_setjmp and dg_longjmp block through Cupid's x86 model. Two seed compiles
agree on those three objects. All 83 Doom and platform sources use .cc and
compile through the checked seed. The wrapper freezes the selected source and
all 289 profile headers before it publishes an object. Its exact source
memberships make a missing, renamed, or unlisted root fail the normal build.

It scans visible .c and .cc files beneath the Doom tree before publication, so
a legacy .c file or an unlisted .cc file also fails. Four-CPU e1000 and RTL8139
boots pass the no-WAD, missing-IWAD recovery, shell-survival, and full frontier
checks. Gameplay still needs a staged WAD.

Checked-seed CupidC represents the GNU returns_twice function attribute. A
marked function must remain a direct call target. Supported calls use four-byte
cdecl arguments and may return void or any nonaggregate type. A live-prefix
call protects its surrounding expression with call-owned frame slots, then
restores those words after cleanup. Such a site fails if any returns-twice
continuation can reach it again. A marked call with no live prefix may repeat.
Aggregate, wide-integer, and wider-than-four-byte floating arguments, aggregate
results, and marked-function pointer conversions fail explicitly.

The corrected dg_setjmp form records the caller stack after a normal return.
Active dg_setjmp uses that 31-byte ESP-plus-four form and returns_twice.
dg_longjmp, dg_exit, and dg_abort use noreturn; dg_longjmp remains 38 bytes.
A decoder-driven i386 oracle models both returns. The guest dglibc_test also
executes the active jump and exit landings, then runs two quit and two error
cycles. It checks LIFO callbacks, error filtering, and cleanup between shell
sessions.

The active config and game-save routines use checked temporary streams and
native same-mount rename beneath /home/doom. HomeFS and RamFS reject busy
replacement. Block-cache reads stage incoming bytes before changing a victim.
FAT16 distinguishes resource and I/O failures from a missing file, blocks
replacement of live entries, enforces one-level 8.3 paths, and publishes a
flushed replacement or deletion before releasing the detached chain. HomeFS
rejects corrupt containers and duplicate mounts, reserves HOMEFS.SYS against
raw FAT writes, and supports nested batches with one outer durable publish.
The asset-free self-test covers these paths with test-only files. A staged WAD
is still required for gameplay and menu-driven save/load proof.
>endtree

>h2 Feature 5: Print Builtin
>tree open Feature 5: Print Builtin
Language-level builtins:
  Print(fmt, ...)
  PrintLine("text")

Format support includes practical console cases used by CupidC demos:
  %d %u %x %X %s %c %p %%

With P1 FPU support, printf also handles %f, %e, %g.

Demo: bin/feature5_print_builtin.cc
[Run feature5_print_builtin](shell:feature5_print_builtin)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 6: #exe Blocks
>tree open Feature 6: #exe Blocks
#exe is implemented.

Behavior:
- JIT mode: #exe blocks are transformed to hidden functions and executed once
  before normal entry.
- AOT mode: #exe blocks are skipped with warning.

Demo: bin/feature6_exe.cc
[Run feature6_exe](shell:feature6_exe)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 7: new / del
>tree open Feature 7: new / del
Supported forms:
  new T
  new T[n]
  del p;

Lowering behavior:
- new: kmalloc + zero-init
- del: kfree + pointer reset to null

Demo: bin/feature7_new_del.cc
[Run feature7_new_del](shell:feature7_new_del)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 8: reg / noreg
>tree open Feature 8: reg / noreg
reg and noreg are accepted as storage hints.

The parser accepts both keywords as semantic no-op hints in the current
compiler backend.

Demo: bin/feature8_reg_noreg.cc
[Run feature8_reg_noreg](shell:feature8_reg_noreg)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 9: Absolute Addressing Sugar
>tree open Feature 9: Absolute Addressing Sugar
Literal-to-pointer style is supported for systems-style memory access.

Typical pattern:
  U8 *v = (U8*)0xB8000;

Demo: bin/feature9_abs_addr.cc
[Run feature9_abs_addr](shell:feature9_abs_addr)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 10: TempleOS-Style Shell REPL
>tree open Feature 10: TempleOS-Style Shell REPL
Primary entry point:
  type CupidC directly at the normal shell prompt

TempleOS-style behavior:
- Shell input is treated as CupidC first, then falls back to normal command dispatch.
- Statements and expressions persist in one long-lived REPL compiler state.
- The last result is available as: ans
- Successful evaluations are reported by the next prompt, like TempleOS.
- Multi-line blocks are supported with brace balancing:
    /home> U32 Add(U32 a, U32 b) {
    ..>   return a + b;
    ..> }
- Failed snippets are rolled back and do not corrupt prior REPL state.
- Forward-call fixups remain in the persistent REPL compiler state until a later
  definition resolves them.
- Shell command fallback still works for non-C inputs like: help, ls /home

HolyC-style note:
- End statements and expressions with semicolons.
- Zero-argument REPL-defined functions can be called as: Foo;

Compatibility commands:
  cc /path/to/file.cc
  cupidc /path/to/file.cc

Demo: bin/feature10_repl.cc
[Run feature10_repl](shell:feature10_repl)
>endtree

>h2 Feature 11: Ternary Operator
>tree open Feature 11: Ternary Operator
The ternary conditional operator is supported:
  condition ? true_expr : false_expr

Example:
  U32 x = 5;
  U32 result = (x > 3) ? 100 : 0;

Demo: bin/feature11_ternary.cc
[Run feature11_ternary](shell:feature11_ternary)
>endtree

>h2 Features 12-16: Float / Double / SIMD / libm / FPU ASM
>tree open Features 12-16: FPU and SSE
These features were added in P1. See 14FPU-SSE.CTXT for full reference.

Summary:
- Feature 12: scalar float arithmetic, casts, element access
- Feature 13: double, transcendentals, unary signs, comparisons, and truth
- Feature 14: float4 / double2 SIMD + intrinsics
- Feature 15: libm (25 operations, 50 symbols with f-variants)
- Feature 16: CupidC inline asm using SSE + x87

Types added: float, double, float4, double2
Intrinsics: _mm_*_ps (17 variants), _mm_*_pd (11 variants)

Unary plus accepts char, int, float, and double. Unary minus uses integer NEG
for char and int. For float and double it changes only the IEEE-754 sign bit,
so negative zero and payload bits stay exact. Other operand types fail with a
clear diagnostic, and the next REPL expression can still compile.
The boot frontier accepts that deliberate diagnostic only during the
feature13_double check. A stale or repeated copy still fails the run.
The same guest checks four float or double to unsigned-word conversions on
both sides of 2^31, signed and high-bit unsigned %=, and one evaluation of a
side-effecting array destination. The boot gate requires
[feature13-unsigned] PASS conversions=4 remainders=2 once=1 and rejects the
separate conversion and remainder failure markers.

All six float and double comparisons return a normalized int. Matching
widths compare directly, while a mixed pair compares as double. Signed zero
compares equal to positive zero. If either operand is NaN, only != is true.

Float and double values can control !, if, ?:, while, for, and do-while
directly. Both signed zero encodings are false. Finite nonzero values,
infinities, and NaNs are true.

All kernel bindings carry their declared result type. The private compiler
uses that metadata to keep returned integers, pointers, floats, and doubles
in later expressions and conditions.

Each binding's advertised argument count must match its C declaration.
kmalloc takes one unsigned size and uses a kernel wrapper that supplies stable
debug provenance. New and array new return zeroed storage through that typed
binding. Del releases the storage through kfree and resets the pointer.

Scalar float and double lvalues support prefix and postfix ++ and --.
Postfix expressions return the old payload, while all four forms store the
one-unit update. The same path handles locals, parameters, globals, pointer
dereferences, fixed-array elements, and scalar fields reached through direct,
pointer, or indexed-record members. The destination expression runs once.

One-dimensional fixed float and double array symbols preserve their element
type. Global, automatic, block-static, and persistent REPL arrays use
four-byte or eight-byte strides with indirect SSE loads and stores. Plain
indexed assignment converts from represented integer and floating sources,
while +=, -=, *=, and /= use the element width. One-, two-, and
three-dimensional fixed arrays retain their remaining row stride, and
sizeof(array[index]) reports that row without running the index. Depth-one
floating pointers preserve their pointee width through address expressions,
returns, array parameters, dereference, subscripts, scaled direct pointer
updates, and assignment. Structure and class objects, arrays, and pointers
retain scalar floating fields and one-dimensional fixed floating field arrays.
Bounds and dimension products are checked before storage is reserved. Deeper
floating pointers, pointer-to-array types, and
assignment through a pointer-valued floating field subscript
remain unsupported.

Matching float4 and double2 values support direct +, -, *, and /. Fixed arrays
with one, two, or three dimensions work in global, automatic, block-static,
and persistent REPL storage. Each vector leaf uses 16 bytes and unaligned-safe
packed moves. Declared rank remains independent of byte stride, so a unit inner
extent still needs its final subscript. Indexed assignment and +=, -=, *=, and
/= preserve lane access and evaluate every destination index once. Row and
vector sizeof do not evaluate their indexes. Every direct operator keeps the
written left value in the machine destination. Prefix and postfix ++ and --
work on modifiable direct vectors and fully indexed leaves. Direct automatic,
global, block-static, and persistent REPL vectors use complete 16-byte storage.
Each indexed destination is evaluated once. Prefix returns the stored vector,
and postfix returns the exact old payload. Const qualification is retained
through typedef aliases. Const direct vectors and fixed-array leaves remain
readable. Plain and arithmetic compound assignment, plus prefix and postfix ++
and --, are rejected before a store. Lane updates, computed vector updates,
SIMD pointers, record fields, new, array parameters, and row values remain
unsupported. MIN and MAX intrinsics keep the second input for NaN and
equal signed-zero cases. For a both-NaN ADD or MUL, the processor or emulator
may preserve either input payload. Vector expressions reject incomplete rows
instead of letting them escape as untyped pointers.

Private CupidC uses one cdecl layout for direct functions and methods.
Integers, pointers, function pointers, float, and the implicit method self
value occupy four bytes. Double occupies eight bytes with its low word at the
lower address. A fixed float4 or double2 parameter occupies 16 bytes and
returns through XMM0. Arguments are evaluated from left to right, then arranged
at increasing addresses in source order. Callees use MOVUPS for vector slots,
and callers reclaim the complete outgoing area. Slots are packed at four-byte
granularity; the private boundary does not promise 16-byte call-site alignment.
A fixed SIMD prefix may precede scalar variadic values. SIMD variadic tails,
unprototyped SIMD calls, and signature-erased function-pointer SIMD calls are
rejected. A named block-local function pointer with an explicit prototype
retains fixed parameter types, variadic state, and its result. The indirect
call uses the same conversions and four-, eight-, or sixteen-byte cdecl slots,
including SIMD arguments and XMM0 results. A file-scope function-pointer
typedef retains its result, fixed parameters, record identities, prototype
state, and variadic boundary. A free-function or Cupid class method parameter
declared through that typedef gets the same signature. A
declaration-initialized automatic object gets an independent copy for every
declarator. A file object declared directly with the typedef also keeps the
signature across null or compatible function initialization, checked plain
assignment, indirect call, and clearing. A defined function address is written
into initialized data, while a later definition receives an absolute data
patch. A direct &function address uses the same signature check, data write or
patch, automatic initialization, and checked assignment as the plain function
designator. Runtime initialization and assignment accept &(function) and
nested grouping. JIT and private AOT calls use the retained signatures. A
structure or class field declared through a typedef or raw function-pointer
declarator keeps the signature for checked stores, copies, null checks,
clearing, and direct postfix calls. Fixed and variadic arguments use cdecl.
Represented scalar, pointer, floating, and SIMD results retain their normal
types. Nested and indexed field designators are evaluated once, and a real
field takes precedence over same-named method sugar. Empty (), alias chains,
raw method parameters, void-pointer forms,
and aggregate callback results remain unsupported or signature-erased.
A named raw callback file object and direct free-function parameter retain the
parsed signature without a typedef. The file object uses the same data write or
patch, and the parameter uses the same cdecl slot and arity checks. The private
raw-signature pool owns those parameter signatures. ADR 0315 records the raw
forms, and ADR 0319 records direct explicit
function addresses. ADR 0321 records typedef-backed record and class fields,
ADR 0324 records grouped runtime addresses, and ADR 0325 records raw fields and
direct postfix field calls.

Raw one-dimensional function-pointer arrays retain the parsed signature when
they use file, block-static, or persistent REPL storage. The block-static raw
scalar form shares this data allocation, initializer, and patch path. A fixed
array needs a positive checked bound. An omitted bound is inferred from a
nonempty braced initializer; an unsized declaration without that initializer
fails. Fixed storage is cleared before its elements are written, so an omitted
initializer and missing trailing entries stay null. An initializer cannot
exceed the declared bound, and each non-null element must match the array
signature. Known function addresses are stored immediately. Later definitions
use absolute data patches.

An indexed element keeps the callback result, fixed parameters, record
identities, prototype state, and variadic boundary through assignment, copying,
argument conversion, and calls. Direct indexed calls and the explicit
(*callbacks[index])(...) spelling use the same typed cdecl path, with one
evaluation of the index. The private source contract follows Doom's active
kernel/doom/src/f_wipe.cc declaration, static int (*wipes[])(int, int, int),
and its wipeno*3 phase dispatch. The
production Doom object remains a checked-seed hosted CupidC output.

Fixed-size automatic raw callback arrays use cleared four-byte local frame
slots and retain their signature through brace initialization, indexed stores,
copies, and calls. Unsized automatic arrays, raw callback array parameters,
raw callback array fields, and multidimensional raw callback arrays remain unsupported.
Typedef-backed fixed callback arrays on record fields keep their retained
signature under ADR 0328. ADR 0330 records the data-backed raw array and shared
block-static scalar rules. ADR 0351 records the fixed automatic form.
Feature 13
retains ten calls through one mixed-width tolerance helper. Feature 14 adds six
nested pass-by-value vector
calls and named SIMD callback forms. ADR 0301 records the earlier named-local
boundary.
Plain function initializers and direct &function addresses must match the
retained signature. An explicit
void-pointer cast remains the deliberate erasure path. Local callback copies
are checked, later addresses are fixed up, and provisional signatures must
match their definitions. Compatible conditional selection checks every named
arm. A represented integer constant expression that evaluates to zero is a
null initializer. Unary signs, integer casts, arithmetic, character zero, and
sizeof(int) - 4 are represented. Runtime zero is not. Null arms are neutral for
explicit erasure, while every non-null object-pointer arm must be cast. Failed
functions and methods restore emitted state, patches, signatures, labels, and
control nesting. A failed source also restores touched prototypes, definitions,
kernel bindings, and a reused __start. The implicit thunk is typed void(void).

kernel/fs/iso9660.cc supplies the active scalar case with susp_entry_cb, whose
three fixed parameters are preserved when susp_walk calls it. The feature14
source adds a float4 callback typedef and uses it on a free-function parameter,
a file object, a declaration-initialized automatic object, a Cupid class method
parameter, and a record field. Focused JIT and private AOT tests execute these
forms, including the 16-byte float4 slot and XMM0 result. They also cover arity,
variadic promotion, record identity, incompatible signatures, null constant
expressions, explicit erasure, forward definitions, and same-state recovery.

The four-vCPU e1000 feature14 guest contract requires ordered direct-call,
named-callback, typedef-parameter, typedef-global, automatic-callback, and
callback-field evidence. The retained paths must print
`[feature14-callback-typedef] PASS float4=4 calls=1`,
`[feature14-callback-global] PASS float4=4 initialized=1 assigned=1`
`cleared=1 calls=2`,
`[feature14-callback-automatic] PASS local=4 method=4 calls=2`, and
`[feature14-callback-field] PASS stored=1 copied=1 cleared=1 float4=4 calls=1`
in that order. The host contract proves the source and required marker. A
focused four-vCPU QEMU boot also observes the field marker. The command then
prints the overall feature14 PASS and finishes with CupidC JIT completion.

Function-pointer typedef declarations remain file-scope, allow one alias per
declaration, and share a sixteen-entry table. Direct record and array results
are rejected. Record-pointer results retain identity.

Nested function-pointer parameters keep child signature handles. A raw child
uses an interned raw-signature handle, while a child named by a direct callback
typedef keeps that typedef handle. The parser and compatibility checker follow
those handles through further callback parameters. Equivalent prototyped raw
and typedef-backed forms are compatible when their result, record-pointer
identity, fixed parameter list, and variadic boundary agree. The existing
unprototyped compatibility rule remains in force. The structural check applies
at each retained child.

Every nested callback is still one four-byte i386 cdecl argument. Its handle is
compile-time metadata. Nesting is bounded at sixteen signature levels. The
backing pool holds 33 signatures; the active kernel callback uses one and
leaves 32 for source declarations. A rejected function, complete source,
or persistent REPL unit restores the child handles and raw pool together with
symbols, data patches, and emitted state.

The active set_icon_drawer binding uses
void (*p_icon_set_drawer)(int, void (*)(int, int)). Private CupidC retains the
outer setter and inner drawer shapes. Function pointers cannot return records
or arrays, and raw callback array parameters remain unsupported. Raw Cupid
class method parameters, callback alias chains, and aggregate callback results
also remain outside the retained path. Checked-seed hosted CupidC still owns
the production kernel/lang/cupidc.cc object.

Reviewed native kernel bindings publish complete fixed or variadic metadata
through the existing function-pointer signature representation. Direct
SYM_KERNEL calls reuse the ordinary typed conversion, cdecl layout, cleanup,
arity, default promotion, and result paths. Console, string, port, and all 50
libm bindings form the first reviewed set. Other registrations use the named
legacy result-only path and keep their previous source-width arguments. The
active set_icon_drawer binding publishes its retained void(int, int) child
through this seam. ADR 0333 records the nested binding.

Direct functions and methods with parsed fixed parameter types convert
represented integer, char, float, and double arguments to the declared cdecl
slot before the call. Represented pointer categories and integer null forms can
fill a pointer slot. A represented object pointer can fill a fixed int or
unsigned int slot as one unchanged i386 word. Narrow and floating destinations
remain rejected, and the existing represented pointer-category rule is
unchanged. The unchanged /bin/ctxt.cc call to ctxt_parse_action reaches this
coercion boundary. That file is an include fragment. /bin/notepad.cc includes
it completely and passes private AOT compilation. A parsed variadic tail widens float to double and promotes
char to int. Calls without fixed parameter metadata keep their source-width
slots. Character operands undergo
integer promotion and use the scalar integer conversion path when mixed with
floating values or explicitly cast. Fresh subscript metadata prevents a
pointer result from inheriting an earlier array stride.

Decimal float and double tokens use a fixed 1536-bit integer workspace. The
compiler forms the exact decimal ratio and rounds once to the requested IEEE
width with ties going to even. An f suffix selects binary32 before rounding.
The path covers subnormals, finite limits, infinity, and signed zero. Numeric
tokens may contain up to 95 characters, including the suffix. Longer tokens
and missing exponent digits receive focused errors. Hexadecimal floating and
long double literals remain unsupported.

The Browser's JavaScript numeric tables use the fixed double-array path.
browser --selftest exercises that path without opening a window. Its 26 result
fields cover decimal and radix literals, numeric separators, whole-string
number conversion with ECMAScript whitespace, primitive equality, UTF-16
string relations, negative zero, NaN and infinity, division, IEEE remainder,
%=, and string +=. Long concatenation and pool exhaustion share the string
field. Compound member and index writes keep the reference selected before the
right side runs, even when that side changes the receiver or key. A 1,100-write
loop checks that assignment leaves the value stack balanced. Ten malformed
forms must report their expected errors before a valid script proves recovery.
String interning fails before publishing a token, binding, property, DOM
value, or global, and failed global setup blocks queued scripts. Native
function IDs survive user-function arguments and returns. Array index writes
grow the unsigned length lane through index 4,294,967,294. Direct length
assignment fails explicitly, while 4,294,967,295 remains an ordinary property.
Finite formatting covers 4,294,967,295, 1e20, and 1e-7 without a
signed 32-bit narrowing.

Private CupidC joins adjacent ordinary string tokens in one data object for
automatic expressions, file-scope pointer initializers, and persistent REPL
declarations. Each token may decode to 1,023 bytes. The joined string can use
the remaining private data section. Longer tokens and data exhaustion fail
without truncation.

Private CupidC accepts both anonymous and tagged structure typedefs. One field
layout path serves both forms, and the typedef table keeps the structure index
through alias chains and structure-pointer aliases. The same metadata persists
across REPL lines. A missing alias and an incomplete by-value field receive
focused errors. Fixed array products, cumulative record layout, final
alignment, REPL data capacity, and cumulative local frames are checked before
allocation. Signed constant arithmetic rejects overflow, while an unsigned
operand wraps modulo 2^32. Integer tokens stop at UINT32_MAX, hexadecimal
literals require at least one digit, and the suffix counts inside the
95-character limit. Failed REPL
input restores complete records, including a previously declared forward tag.
Member address expressions select their storage without reading its value.
The &record.field form starts at the object, while &pointer->field loads the
pointed-to record before applying the field offset. Missing fields keep the
unknown-struct-field diagnostic.

Private typedef declarations may publish several value or pointer aliases.
One-dimensional array aliases keep their complete storage in automatic,
global, block-static, record, class, and persistent REPL declarations. An
array member keeps its complete sizeof result and the structure identity of
its elements through direct or pointer access. Reads and assignments may
continue after the index, including when the outer record came from an array.

Unsigned 32-bit values keep their type through objects, calls, conditional
arms, sizeof, scalar returns, and conversion to float or double. Comparisons,
division, remainder, and right shift use unsigned i386 behavior. /=, %=, and
>>= keep that rule and evaluate their destination once. Values in C's defined
interval convert back from float or double through casts, initialization,
assignment, fixed arguments, and returns. Forty kernel
bindings with uint32_t, size_t, or swap_handle_t results publish that lane;
narrower unsigned results keep integer promotion.

The libm bindings also retain their fixed parameter widths. A base function
such as sqrt publishes double(double), while its f-suffixed partner publishes
float(float). Integer arguments convert before the call, and a float widens for
a declared double parameter. Arity and type failures leave the compiler ready
for a later valid program.

