>h2 Current Bootstrap Boundary
>tree open Current Bootstrap Boundary
Normal `all` uses poisoned-host Cupid tooling. Its exact artifact-size policy
covers sixteen paths: four OS artifacts, six Linux seed images, and six
Windows seed images. CUPSIZE2 gives the CupidC-built policy contract the
Windows manifest, Linux parent digest, and six regular-file size and digest
observations. The contract validates the Windows target, provenance, exact
inventory, and observed bytes. The focused semantic-contract, checked-runner,
and independent-policy modules contain 22, 16, and 13 tests, for 51 total.
They pass with four existing platform-specific skips. The host bootstrap log
records the complete source-consistent normal build, final checked publication,
and private four-vCPU proof for this increment. Exact artifact and runtime
measurements stay there because this manual is an image input.

The active Linux and Windows v2 cohorts are complete six-tool trust units.
CupidBuild is checked in both and owns four normal guarded assembly
publications: two objects and two raw images. It also runs the 186 direct
CupidObj recipes and both normal kernel links selected by Make.
Their CupidDis images carry local-target validation for raw images,
relocatable objects, and static linked executables. Active-source tests cover
all nine bootloader targets and all four trampoline targets. Production also
checks active CupidASM objects and both kernel ELFs before publication. The
promoted images also validate static ELF entry points and defined function
symbols against decoded instruction starts. Production selects that rule for
both kernel ELFs before flattening.
Separate Linux and Windows seed fixtures exercise positive and negative cases.

The private in-kernel callback parser and AOT writer remain separate from the
standalone toolchain compiler seeds. Private CupidC retains file-scope callback
typedef signatures on free-function parameters, Cupid class method parameters,
declaration-initialized automatic objects, and direct file objects. A direct
function address keeps the same callback signature and data patch as a plain
function designator. A direct file object supports null initialization, checked
plain assignment, indirect calls, and clearing. Structure and class fields
declared directly with a callback typedef retain the signature for checked
stores, named copies, null checks, and clearing. Raw callback fields retain the
same signature. Direct postfix calls through raw or typedef-backed fields use
typed cdecl conversion and evaluate the designator once. Data-backed raw
callback arrays and block-static raw callback scalars use the ADR 0330 path.
Nested raw or typedef-backed callback parameters retain child handles and are
checked recursively. Alias chains, aggregate results, raw Cupid class method
parameters, and `void *` forms remain outside that typed path. ADR 0325 records
the field-call boundary.

The active set_icon_drawer binding has one four-byte nested drawer parameter.
Its private signature graph uses the sixteen-level depth bound and one record
from the 33-entry backing pool. Source retains a 32-entry budget. Rollback
restores both parent and child handles. The standalone checked seeds do not
contain this private parser; checked-seed hosted CupidC remains the production
owner of kernel/lang/cupidc.cc.

The Toolchain manifest has a Cupid-built author and verifier, while Python keeps
descriptor-pinned filesystem capture and atomic publication. Linux runs the
author as a static ELF. Windows runs a validated native PE built by the same
Linux stage-four CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD producers with the checked
Windows runtime, startup, and exact imports. The publisher uses
a private pending bootstrap path and gives the author all 62 raw stage pairs
before Python performs its independent comparisons. A valid schema-v3
publication binds exactly 22 artifacts, 75 publication inputs, 58 bootstrap
inputs, and 17 object comparisons. Both checked Python contract launchers put
this checkout ahead of installed packages before importing `tools`. The direct
contract passes 40 tests in 51.723 seconds, the publisher passes 65 in 7.258
seconds, and the pinned runner passes 25 in 32.773 seconds. The host bootstrap
log records the exact publication identity and completion evidence.

Audit generation and checked reproduction pass. The audit records 747 active
inputs, 452 transforms, 255 feature requirements,
and 27 accounted unreachable files. Ownership participation is CupidC 250,
CupidObj 192, CupidASM 9, CupidLD 9, CupidDis 9, and CupidBuild 192. Python
participates in 260. Four transforms run Cupid-built contracts, and none is
Python-only. The host bootstrap log records
the post-manual-freeze poisoned build and strong private four-vCPU e1000
frontier. Exact OS identities and runtime measurements stay there because this
manual is an image input.
>endtree

>h2 Practical Notes
>tree open Practical Notes
1) CupidC is kernel-integrated; C and GNU-extension coverage remains incomplete.
2) Keep the source's natural modules and abstractions. When active code reaches
   a compiler limit, extend CupidC instead of simplifying the program.
3) Separate compilation and multi-file linking remain toolchain work. Focused
   tests should isolate each new capability while that support is built.
4) #pragma once is supported for include-guard patterns.
5) The private compiler accepts __attribute__((...)) mainly for source
   compatibility. The shared bootstrap compiler gives its documented entity
   attributes real declaration or call behavior and rejects unknown forms.
>endtree

>h2 Active Six-Tool Bootstrap Lineage
>tree
The active v2 checked-seed format includes CupidBuild with the five existing
tools. Both manifests bind revision
`a4eee4c2c4b8f1cbb7ca22fbe7688f5958912e4f`, 58 source inputs, and snapshot
`a2e8e5c97672c2d0bd8ba4f4166860cc9686a1838cefeab8bc46d5b1c9fbe09d`.

The Linux plan has SHA-256
`52dd857bcb74e079e7e2eec45eaa90a0a0838ad2f4e817bebc35c9904efbecbd`.
Its 6,602-byte manifest has SHA-256
`f1bee18b9b1506ff5a665e76d57d028702ae7c701c4e9d432ed4b87c68ee258b`.
The Windows native plan has SHA-256
`f9dce66230a693de9d9d0e60127a4a6c44ea465989f381c995086bfe723cff14`.
Its 2,852-byte manifest has SHA-256
`917817122a36331a0ec77ba06d6ce40a8eacacc4224d8ed468d8d77272b8b974`
and pairs to the exact Linux manifest bytes.

Candidate proof and promoted-seed self-consumption pass on Linux and native
Windows. All six initial images match stage two. The behavior inventories are
25/6/32 on Linux and 14/6/19 on Windows. The normal ISR, context-switch,
bootloader, and SMP-trampoline recipes use CupidBuild directly, as do the 186
ordinary CupidObj recipes and both normal kernel links. Python participates in
the remaining 260
transforms. ADR 0357 records the raw recipe transfer, ADR 0360 records the
checked runner, ADR 0361 records its paired seed promotion, and ADR 0362
records the direct CupidObj handoff. ADR 0363 records the kernel-link handoff.
>endtree

